content.text:
Now resizeCanvas. Current:
```
const dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, 2);
canvas.width = Math.round(w*dpr)...
```
Change to include renderScale and mobile cap:
```
const nativeDpr = window.devicePixelRatio || 1;
const cap = IS_ANDROID ? 1.5 : 2;
const dpr = Math.max(0.75, Math.min(nativeDpr, cap) * state.renderScale);
```
But on desktop with dpr1 and renderScale 1 → dpr1. On retina desktop dpr2 cap2. On Android dpr3 cap1.5. Adaptive reduces 0.1 steps.

Note: `view.unit` based on CSS w/h unaffected. Good.

Also `ctx.setTransform(dpr...)` etc.

Then loop with fixed step, fps meter, adaptive downscale.

Important: `render(time)` is called with `time = now/1000`. updateFx should probably run each frame with a dt (frame dt) rather than per step, but fixed step is fine and consistent. If fps is low (e.g. 30), accumulator does 2 steps per frame, so updateFx twice → correct simulation.

Clamp accumulator to avoid spiral: max 6 steps per frame (0.1s). If remaining > 0.1, drop.

FPS meter:
```
let frames = 0;
let fpsAt = performance.now();
...
frames += 1;
if (now - fpsAt >= 500) {
  const fps = Math.min(240, Math.round(frames * 1000 / (now - fpsAt)));
  state.fps = fps;
  frames = 0;
  fpsAt = now;
  if (hudFps) {
    hudFps.textContent = `${fps} FPS`;
    hudFps.classList.toggle('is-warn', fps < 55 && fps >= 40);
    hudFps.classList.toggle('is-low', fps < 40);
  }
  // adaptive quality: only while running
  if (state.running && fps < 50 && state.renderScale > 0.7 && now - state.qualityAt > 2000) {
    state.renderScale = Math.max(0.7, state.renderScale - 0.1);
    state.qualityAt = now;
    resizeCanvas();
  }
}
```
But resizeCanvas rebuilds backdrop randomly and resets stars etc. That may cause a visual jump once. Acceptable. To avoid repeated, threshold 0.7.

Also if fps > 58 and renderScale < 1, could restore; but might oscillate. Only downscale.

Issue: headless fps may be ~10; renderScale drops to 0.7 → still 10, but no further. Fine.

Also target "on 60fps" — show "60 FPS" initial and actual measured.

`state.last` handling: when leaving, reset acc. In startLoop set `state.acc = 0`.

dt clamp: use `Math.min((now - state.last)/1000, 0.25)`. On a tab switch, drop excess.

Pause handling: when paused/running false, should we still accumulate and step? We update only if running; but updateFx currently runs always. updateFx during loader is fine but no fx. During pause (kit open) fx still updates; okay. To avoid accumulating while paused, we still consume steps; fine.

However when `state.running` false (loader), updateGame isn't called; acc consumed. Fine.

Loop rewrite:
```
function startLoop() {
  if (state.raf) cancelAnimationFrame(state.raf);
  state.last = performance.now();
  state.acc = 0;
  let frames = 0;
  let fpsAt = state.last;

  const frame = (now) => {
    if (!state.open) { state.raf = 0; return; }
    let dt = (now - state.last) / 1000;
    state.last = now;
    if (dt > 0.25) dt = 0.25;
    state.acc += dt;

    let steps = 0;
    while (state.acc >= STEP && steps < 6) {
      if (state.running && !state.paused && !game.ended) updateGame(STEP);
      updateFx(STEP);
      state.acc -= STEP;
      steps += 1;
    }
    if (state.acc > STEP * 6) state.acc = 0;

    render(now / 1000);

    frames += 1;
    const span = now - fpsAt;
    if (span >= 500) {
      const fps = Math.max(1, Math.min(240, Math.round((frames * 1000) / span)));
      state.fps = fps;
      frames = 0;
      fpsAt = now;
      if (hudFps) {
        hudFps.textContent = `${fps} FPS`;
        ...
      }
      if (state.running && !state.paused && fps < 50 && now - state.qualityAt > 2500 && state.renderScale > 0.7) {
        ...
      }
    }
    state.raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame);
  };
  state.raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame);
}
```

Wait: calling resizeCanvas from inside rAF callback may be heavy but fine.

Important: `render(now/1000)` uses time for animations; with fixed step render time can be now. Fine.

But gameplay animation (player run phase) uses `time` for leg swing etc. With 60fps fixed step, visual updates smooth.

One nuance: capping at 60fps on 120Hz screens — rAF still 120, we render every frame (120fps) but simulate at 60. That's okay; the request "on 60fps" could mean cap at 60. We can render every rAF; on high refresh it will show >60fps. To lock 60, skip render when not enough time? But rendering 120fps is fine, arguably better. The HUD would show 120fps, conflicting with "60fps target". Hmm. "on 60fps" likely means "run at 60fps". Capping render to 60 on 120Hz yields stable 60. Implement:
```
let lastRender = 0;
...
const renderEvery = 1000 / TARGET_FPS; // 16.67
